                <pb facs="0543" n="441" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg441"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.114 Bodo/8: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in Norway</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Biddle</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-11-20T17:00:00-05:00">November 20,
                            1935—5 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>18. Department’s No. 17, November 18, 5 p.m.<anchor n="77" xml:id="d320-f1"/> If
                    you see no objection, request Consulate General to inform Shipping Company
                    Nordfart (Erling Sannes), Bodo, Norway, owners of vessel <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bodo</hi>, that it is expected that <hi rendition="#italic">Bodo</hi> will
                    load large quantity alcohol at Antwerp for discharge on high seas into smuggling
                    vessels; that W. A. Shaw, Managing Director of Shaw Steamship Company, Limited,
                    of Halifax, by whom <hi rendition="#italic">Bodo</hi> is said to have been
                    chartered, has for several years been connected with the ownership or operation
                    of vessels engaged in smuggling; that Shaw’s former shipping agents in Antwerp
                    are said to have requested from his representative guarantee that cargo would be
                    landed at destination, which was refused, and they gave up agency; that by
                    allowing vessel to be used in connection with smuggling they are apparently
                    violating Norwegian law of June 25, 1926. If pressed for reasons for belief
                    cargo will be smuggled state that during past few months four other steamers
                    chartered by Shaw have discharged cargoes on high seas into smuggling vessels.
                    Telegraph reply.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
